      'The Goods and Services Tax compliance calendar for Financial Year 2026-27 is packed with over 40 filing deadlines that every registered business must track. Missing even a single return can trigger late fees of ₹50 per day (₹20 for nil returns) under CGST and SGST combined, capping at ₹10,000 per return. This guide lays out every deadline so you never miss one.',
      'GSTR-1, the outward supply return, is due on the 11th of the following month for businesses with turnover above ₹5 crore. Smaller businesses under the QRMP scheme file quarterly, with the Invoice Furnishing Facility (IFF) available for the first two months of each quarter. Getting GSTR-1 right is critical because your buyers\' input tax credit depends on it.',
      'GSTR-3B, the summary return where you pay your tax liability, is due on the 20th of the following month for monthly filers. Quarterly filers under QRMP get staggered dates — the 22nd or 24th depending on the state. The key here is to reconcile your GSTR-3B figures with GSTR-2B (auto-generated from suppliers\' GSTR-1) before filing, to avoid ITC mismatches.',
      'The annual return GSTR-9 is due by December 31, 2027 for FY 2026-27. Businesses with turnover exceeding ₹5 crore must also file GSTR-9C, the reconciliation statement, by the same date. These annual filings require meticulous reconciliation of your books with your monthly returns — start the process early, ideally by October.',
      'Late fees are not the only risk. Persistent non-filing can lead to GST registration suspension under Section 29, which freezes your ability to issue tax invoices and can disrupt your entire supply chain. The department typically issues suspension notices after two consecutive months of non-filing.',
      'Automation is your best defence. Set up filing reminders at least 5 days before each deadline. Use accounting software that pre-fills return data from your invoicing. And consider a compliance management tool like DoAide Comply that sends WhatsApp and email alerts keyed to your specific filing frequency and state.',
      'For businesses under the composition scheme, GSTR-4 (annual) is due by April 30, 2027. The quarterly CMP-08 challan is due on the 18th of the month following each quarter. Composition dealers often overlook CMP-08 because it is a payment challan rather than a return, but late payment attracts interest at 18% per annum.',
