      'Hiring your first employees is a milestone for any startup — but it also triggers payroll compliance obligations that many founders discover only after receiving a notice from the EPFO or ESIC. Provident Fund (PF) and Employee State Insurance (ESI) are mandatory social security contributions, and non-compliance can result in penalties, interest, and even prosecution. Here is what every Indian startup needs to know.',
      'PF registration becomes mandatory when your establishment employs 20 or more persons. However, once registered, you must continue contributing even if the headcount drops below 20. The contribution rate is 12% of basic wages from the employer and 12% from the employee, subject to a wage ceiling of ₹15,000/month for the employer contribution calculation (employees can voluntarily contribute on their full salary). The employer also contributes 0.5% towards EDLI (Employee Deposit Linked Insurance).',
      'ESI registration is mandatory for establishments employing 10 or more persons (20 in some states) where employees earn up to ₹21,000/month. The contribution is 3.25% from the employer and 0.75% from the employee, calculated on gross wages. ESI provides medical benefits, sickness benefits, maternity benefits, and disablement benefits — it is a valuable safety net that employees in the organised sector rely on.',
