      'India has over 6 crore MSMEs that contribute roughly 30% of GDP and 45% of manufacturing output. Yet many operate with minimal awareness of the compliance obligations that apply to them — and more importantly, the obligations that apply to businesses that deal with them. Whether you are an MSME yourself or a company that pays MSMEs, this guide covers what you need to know.',
      'MSME classification is now based on investment in plant & machinery/equipment and annual turnover. Micro: investment up to ₹1 crore, turnover up to ₹5 crore. Small: investment up to ₹10 crore, turnover up to ₹50 crore. Medium: investment up to ₹50 crore, turnover up to ₹250 crore. Registration is done through the Udyam portal (udyam.gov.in) and is free, paperless, and based on self-declaration linked to your Aadhaar and PAN.',
      'The most impactful recent change is Section 43B(h) of the Income Tax Act, effective from FY 2023-24. If your business makes payments to a micro or small enterprise and does not pay within 45 days (or the agreed payment period, if shorter), the expense is disallowed as a deduction. In practice, this means your taxable income increases by the unpaid amount. For a company in the 25% tax bracket, a ₹10 lakh unpaid invoice to an MSME vendor increases your tax by ₹2.5 lakh.',
      'MSME-1 filing: Every company that has outstanding payments to MSMEs beyond 45 days must file Form MSME-1 with the MCA half-yearly. The first return (for Oct–Mar) is due by April 30, and the second (for Apr–Sep) by October 31. Non-filing attracts penalties of up to ₹25,000 on the company and every officer in default, with continuing penalties of ₹5,000/day.',
      'For MSMEs themselves, the compliance burden is lighter but still real. GST registration is mandatory above the threshold, and once registered, monthly or quarterly returns must be filed without fail. TDS obligations kick in when you make specified payments. PF registration is mandatory at 20 employees. Shops & Establishments registration is needed for every premises. Professional tax varies by state.',
      'The Udyam registration itself has compliance implications. It must be updated if your investment or turnover crosses a threshold boundary (micro to small, small to medium). The registration number must be quoted on all invoices, purchase orders, and contracts. Banks and government departments verify Udyam status for MSME lending schemes, priority sector lending, and government procurement preferences.',
      'Payment monitoring is the new frontier. The government is increasingly cracking down on delayed payments to MSMEs through the MSME Samadhaan portal, where MSMEs can file complaints against buyers. The Facilitation Council can award compound interest at 3x the bank rate on delayed payments. Combined with Section 43B(h) disallowance, there is now real financial pain for companies that delay MSME payments.',
